include "signals.patlang"

# ---- live build progress / health, over the signals IPC port ----
#
# A native build is a genuinely long-running job (the self-hosted
# compiler's own ~330-function bundle takes minutes) and it used to run
# completely silently -- no way to tell "assembling unit 200 of 511"
# from "wedged". That opacity has a real, measured cost: one two-hour
# run's phase boundary (assembly finished, linking began) could only be
# recovered AFTER the fact, by noticing the newest per-unit cache file
# had stopped advancing.
#
# Exposed the way this codebase already exposes live process state --
# signals.patlang's claim/poll/reply, the same request/response shape
# signals_demo.patlang's own "status" signal uses. A build holds the
# port and services queries as it works; anything else can ask it, from
# any process, WHILE it runs:
#   let reply = signal_query(x64_build_signal_port(), "x64_status", "")
#
# Lives in its own leaf module, included by BOTH the assembler
# (x64_asm.patlang) and the build orchestrator (x64_compile_unit.
# patlang), because both need to report. It was originally defined
# alongside the orchestrator, which quietly inverted the dependency --
# the assembler is the lower-level module, and calling upward into the
# orchestrator worked only by accident of build_patc1.patlang's
# concatenation order. Anything that included x64_asm.patlang on its own
# died with "host fn 'x64_build_tick' not found". Only signals.patlang
# is depended on here, and that file includes nothing itself, so this
# stays safe to include from anywhere.
make a function called x64_build_signal_port takes none returns port
  return 9410
end

# Concurrent builds are normal here, so a single fixed port is not
# enough: whichever build starts second gets -1 from signal_claim and
# goes silent for its whole run -- and two builds racing is exactly when
# you most want to see what each is doing. Claiming across a small range
# lets several publish at once. Kept deliberately small; a build that
# cannot find a free port in eleven tries simply does not serve, which
# is still never fatal.
make a function called x64_build_signal_port_last takes none returns port
  return 9420
end

make a function called x64_build_status_text takes none returns status
  return "" + get("__vars", "__x64_build_status")
end

# Claimed once per build, walking the port range until one is free.
# Returns the claimed port_id, or -1 if every port in the range is taken
# -- which is a normal, fine outcome: this build then simply never
# serves. Reporting must never block, delay, or fail the actual work.
make a function called x64_build_signal_claim takes none returns port_id
  let port = x64_build_signal_port()
  let last = x64_build_signal_port_last()
  let claimed = 0 - 1
  while (port <= last) and (claimed < 0) do
    let claimed = signal_claim(port)
    let port = port + 1
  end
  set_var("__x64_build_port", "" + claimed)
  return claimed
end

# Records a new phase/position AND services one pending query.
make a function called x64_build_progress takes phase, done, total returns ok
  set_var("__x64_build_status", phase + " " + done + "/" + total + " t_ms=" + now_ms())
  return x64_build_tick()
end

# A bare yield: services one pending query, if any, WITHOUT updating the
# status line. Long-running components call this on a routine basis from
# inside their own loops, so a query gets answered in reasonable time
# even mid-phase rather than only at the phase boundaries where
# x64_build_progress happens to be called -- exactly the gap that left a
# status probe hanging for minutes while the linker ground through one
# uninterrupted loop.
#
# Costs a single non-blocking accept (signal_poll with a 0 timeout
# returns immediately when nobody is asking), so it is cheap enough for
# every few hundred iterations of a hot loop, and free when no port was
# ever claimed.
#
# The guard is `> 0`, deliberately, not `>= 0`: when no build has
# claimed a port the var is unset, and an unset var coerces to 0, which
# `>= 0` happily accepts -- sending signal_poll at port 0 and taking
# down any program that merely INCLUDED this file without running a
# build ("tcp_accept_timeout: no listener on port 0"). A real claimed
# port is always > 0, and signal_claim's own already-held answer is -1,
# so both non-serving cases fall out here.
make a function called x64_build_tick takes none returns ok
  let pid = to_num("" + get("__vars", "__x64_build_port"))
  if pid > 0 then
    let unused = signal_poll(pid, 0)
  end
  return true
end

# Answers a live `signal_query(x64_build_signal_port(), "x64_status", "")`
# from any other process with the current phase/progress line. Top level
# by necessity, not style: a `when` handler registered inside a function
# or method body does not fire -- it has to be declared at file scope to
# be reachable when signal_poll dispatches.
when x64_status do
  signal_reply(x64_build_status_text())
end
